pipeline:

* train models w/ finetune
* use each model in infer_file to get inference results on file by file basis using knockout set of AR regions
* use average to collect results on channel by channel basis to create average mask maps
* use false_color to create color maps   

Training Data

How the 4 CSVs relate and differ:

assets/train_index_surya_1_0.csv — a different dataset entirely. This is the foundation-model (Surya-1.0) input index, not an AR-segmentation label file:

    Columns: ,path,timestep,present — 345,721 rows at 12-minute cadence, 2011-02-15 to 2019-12-31.
    path points to raw SDO NetCDF files (.nc, e.g. 2011/02/20110215_0000.nc) holding the AIA/HMI channels the model consumes as input.
    present (0/1) just flags whether a valid observation exists at that timestep (309,412 present, 36,309 missing).
    Used as data.train_data_path in config.yaml, feeding Surya's own pretraining/embedding pipeline — it has nothing to do with AR masks. (Its sibling valid_index_surya_1_0.csv, not in your list, plays the matching validation role.)

The three surya-bench-ar-segmentation/*.csv files are the downstream AR-segmentation benchmark's label catalogue, all sharing the schema timestamp, file_path, present, hourly cadence, file_path pointing to .h5 mask files (each containing intersection = PIL map and union_with_intersect = the AR footprint mask, the default training target). Together with a test.csv (not in your list), they form a fixed temporal split over 2010–2019 (test covers 2020–2024) documented in GROUND_TRUTH_EXPLAINED.md:
file 	date coverage 	rows 	present=1
train.csv 	15 Feb – 31 Dec, each year 2010–2019 	74,760 	70,823
validation.csv 	15–31 Jan, each year 2010–2019 	3,672 	3,477
leaky_validation.csv 	1–14 Jan and 1–14 Feb, each year 2010–2019 	6,048 	5,753

These are wired into config.yaml as ar_index_train / ar_index_valid (test.csv likewise, though it isn't shipped locally).

Why leaky_validation.csv is separate and called "leaky": it covers the calendar days immediately adjacent to train.csv's start (Feb 15) and wrapping around validation.csv's window (Jan 15–31) — i.e., Jan 1–14 and Feb 1–14. Although no individual timestamp is duplicated across files (verified — zero overlap), active regions rotate and evolve on multi-day timescales, so samples this close to the training window's boundary are temporally correlated with training data, making it an easier/"leaky" validation set rather than a clean held-out one. validation.csv (mid/late January) is the non-leaky held-out split actually used for model selection.

AR-level catalogues (as opposed to the per-timestamp index CSVs above)

These describe *active regions*, one row per HARP, not one row per frame. None are in git; all live in assets/ (a symlink to shared storage). Regenerate any of them with make_holdout_catalog.py — see "Holdout AR catalogs" in README.md.

    harp_catalog_subset.csv — the raw input, and the only one not derived: hourly HARP bounding-box geometry exported from JSOC hmi.Mharp_720s, 2010-05-13 to 2024-12-31, 1,244,172 rows. Columns timestamp, t_rec_tai, harpnum, lat_fwt, lon_fwt, crpix1/2, crsize1/2, lat/lon_min/max. Missing keywords are the literal string MISSING, so it must be read with na_values=["MISSING"]. The test era (2020-2024) holds 496,759 rows over 2,737 HARPs, of which 2,727 have complete geometry.
    unified_catalog.csv — all four split index CSVs concatenated, tagged by a `source` column. Its `test` segment is content-identical to test.csv (43,848 rows, 41,910 present==1, same file_path values), so either can serve as the overlap source.
    harp_catalog_test_full.csv — all 2,727 test-era HARPs, ranked by peak deprojected area.
    holdout_ar_large_from_test.csv — the 88 of the top 100 test-era ARs that deproject cleanly. This is what the uncertainty/inference work actually evaluates on.
    holdout_ar_large_from_training.csv — the 30 training-era ARs chosen to cost the least data to hold out (no validation/leaky overlap, smallest train overlap). Unlike the test-era file this one required real selection: those ARs sit inside the training window, so holding them out means dropping frames.

    holdout_ar_nonflaring_30_from_test.csv — the non-flaring control set: 30 ARs, 10,000-20,000 million km², each with a NOAA region, at most one flare, that flare below C1.0, and nothing in progress at its peak frame. 27 of the 30 have zero flares outright (recover them by filtering n_flares == 0). Built by annotate_flares.py from the band below; adds 7 flare columns after the canonical 14.
    holdout_ar_nonflaring_band_annotated.csv — all 216 band ARs with the same flare columns, kept so the selection criteria can be changed via annotate_flares.py --from-annotated with no network access. Of the 216: 131 have a NOAA region, 85 do not; 112 have zero attributed flares, but only 27 both have a region and are flare-free.
    hek_flares_2020-2024.csv — 95,620 distinct HEK flare events over 2020-2024, cached in 5-day chunks (~349 requests, ~12 min). Reusable for any era or catalog; rebuild with --refresh-cache.

Both holdout_ar_large_* files share one schema, differing only in the trailing <split>_overlap_files column. Ranks are non-contiguous in both because rank is assigned over every AR in the era *before* the deproj/holdout filters — holdout_ar_large_from_test.csv starts at rank 2 because rank 1 (HARP 9475) is a polar limb region tagged deproj: Poor.

Optimizer divergence in the 2016hr regime (2026-10-27)

The first 2016hr member (boot01, run 2026-10-26, wandb f5ksovav, log /efs/bathomas/run_test_2016.log)
diverged. It is neither over- nor undertrained: train and validation loss move together the whole
way, so there is no overfitting gap, and the model had already learned the task after one epoch.

epoch  train loss   val loss   val IoU   pred AR frac (true 0.0064)
  0      0.0111      0.0088    0.5672    0.0074
  1     10.01        0.8355    0.1086    0.0014
  2      0.582       0.2782    0.0793    0.0006
  3      0.0876      0.0414    0.3656    0.0057
  4      1.292       1.2782    0.1071    0.0031

Training BCE stayed under 0.75 for all of epoch 0, then exploded at epoch 1 batch ~685 to a peak
of 930.25 -- saturated wrong-sign logits -- with 33 batches in that epoch over loss 5. Epochs 2-3
partially recovered, epoch 4 blew up again (peak 134.75).

Cause: finetune.py ran Adam at a constant lr 1e-3 with no warmup, no decay and no gradient
clipping, at batch_size 1 against a 0.64%-positive target. optimizer.warm_up_steps and
optimizer.min_lr were present in every config but never read by the code. Step count per epoch is
what tipped it over -- the same lr 1e-3 was stable at 168 steps/epoch (val loss 0.012-0.016
throughout, IoU climbing monotonically), spiky at 672 (run_672.log has an epoch at val loss 121.1,
IoU 0.0000), and chronically divergent at 2016.

Fixed in finetune.py by three opt-in optimizer config keys, so the 168hrs and 672hr configs keep
the constant-LR behaviour their published results were produced under:
  optimizer.lr_schedule     "constant" (default) | "cosine" -- warmup then cosine to min_lr
  optimizer.warm_up_steps   now actually honored, under lr_schedule: cosine
  optimizer.grad_clip_norm  max_norm for clip_grad_norm_; absent/0 disables (the old behaviour)
finetune.py also now writes best.pth per member (best validation IoU) and logs lr and the pre-clip
grad norm, which is the series that would have shown this coming before the loss blew up.

The 2016hr configs were regenerated at lr 2e-4, cosine, 200 warmup steps, grad_clip_norm 1.0 --
see the invocation in make_bootstrap_configs.py's docstring. Regenerating also fixed drift between
members: boot01 had max_epochs 5 and path_experiment under /mnt/efs while boot02-10 had max_epochs
3 and /efs, so the ten were not comparable. All ten now differ only in job_id, path_experiment and
ar_index_train. Pre-regeneration copies are in configs_bootstrap/2016hr.pre-lrfix.20261027/.

Checkpoint status: of /efs/bathomas/ar_seg_bootstrap/2016hr/boot01/epoch_{0..4}.pth, ONLY epoch_0
is usable (IoU 0.5672). epoch_1 through epoch_4 are diverged and epoch_4 -- the one a "use the last
epoch" convention would pick -- predicts less than half the AR area it should. There is no 2016hr
entry in run_bootstrap_inference.py's BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME yet; add one only after a member trains
cleanly under the new settings. Note that the 672hr comment in that table already recorded the same
symptom ("most members then collapse hard") without naming the cause.

672hr_stable: the stabilized 672hr ensemble (created 2026-10-27)

configs_bootstrap/672hr_stable/ + run_bootstrap_ensemble_672hr_stable.sh. The old 672hr set is
untouched and still runnable; this is an additional set, not a replacement.

Why: 8 of the 10 original 672hr members collapse after their peak. Best epochs land at 0-3 of 5
(mean 1.5), so 60-70% of the compute was wasted or harmful:

  boot02  ep1 0.5608 -> ep4 0.0000   (-0.561)
  boot06  ep1 0.6089 -> ep4 0.0572   (-0.552)
  boot03  ep2 0.6169 -> ep4 0.3177   (-0.423)
  boot01  ep2 0.6036 -> ep9 0.4169   (-0.375)
  boot09  ep1 0.5621 -> ep4 0.2720   (-0.341)
  boot04/08/10                       (-0.25 to -0.28)

3.91% of 672hr training batches exceed loss 1.0 and 0.43% exceed 100 (max 655.84) -- the same
divergence as 2016hr, milder. For contrast, 168hr's max training loss over 6,660 batches is 0.75
and *no* batch exceeds 1.0, which is why 168hr was left alone: it is data-limited, not
optimizer-limited (boot01 ran 20 epochs and peaked at epoch 8 with 0.5170, mid-pack among the
10-epoch members).

What changed from 672hr, and only this:
  warm_up_steps  0    -> 70  (~10% of one epoch)
  lr_schedule    -    -> cosine        (warm_up_steps is only honored under cosine)
  grad_clip_norm -    -> 1.0
  max_epochs     5    -> 10            (boot01 was already 10; the drift is normalized)
learning_rate stays 1e-3 deliberately. 672hr reaches the highest IoU of any regime (0.6341)
despite the instability, so the LR magnitude is productive; clipping is the targeted fix for
occasional blow-ups and does not cost convergence speed the way dropping to 2e-4 would.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY DOING THE WORK (measured 2026-10-27, boot01): **not grad clipping.** This entry
originally predicted "grad clipping alone is what has to prevent those collapses," reasoning that
cosine leaves the LR at 9.8e-4 / 9.1e-4 during epochs 1-2 where the old members collapsed. That
prediction is falsified: across 1,350 logged training batches, **0 exceeded the 1.0 clip
threshold** -- max grad norm was 0.570 (epoch 0, during warmup), p99 was 0.099, and the median
fell from 0.017 to 0.007 over the run. Clipping never engaged, so it cannot be credited.

Warmup and/or cosine decay prevented the collapse, and one run cannot separate them. The clip at
1.0 is best read as a safety net that never needed to fire: the old run's spikes reached loss 655
and would have produced gradients orders of magnitude above anything seen here. Leave it in --
it costs nothing and it is the only thing that would catch a genuine explosion -- but do not
attribute the stability to it. If collapses ever recur, lowering learning_rate is the lever.

AMENDED 2026-10-29, now that all ten members have run: "never needed to fire" was true of boot01
but is not true of the ensemble. Over 8,100 logged batches, 4 of 10 members exceeded the clip --
boot08 1.008 (batch 45), boot03 1.410 (b30), boot05 1.427 (b25), boot06 1.585 (b45) -- every one
of them inside epoch 0's 70-step warmup ramp. Logging is every 5th batch, so the true count is
unobserved and plausibly ~5x that. After epoch 0 the maximum over all members and all epochs is
0.150, 6.7x below the threshold. So the conclusion survives in the part that matters -- the
stability of epochs 1-5 cannot be credited to clipping, which is inert there -- but the clip is
doing real work during warmup, and dropping it is no longer free.

The replicate indices, evaluation indices, LoRA rank/alpha, workers and dtype are identical to
the old 672hr set, and checkpoints go to .../ar_seg_bootstrap/672hr_stable/bootNN, so this is a
clean A/B on stability alone.

run_bootstrap_inference.py's regime_defaults() already resolves "672hr_stable" correctly by
naming convention, and --regime is gated on BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME's keys; the entry was added
2026-10-29 from the rerun log (see the holdout-inference section below).

Cost: 672 samples x ~590 MB = ~396 GB/epoch, ~4.0 TB/member, ~40 TB for all 10 at 10 epochs.
Regenerate with --max-epochs 5 for the ~20 TB version.

CAVEAT (RESOLVED 2026-10-29 -- see "672hr_stable holdout inference" below): if this ensemble
supersedes the old 672hr one, the sections 6/7 analysis (ICC, reliability, the 2.28x
error/uncertainty ratio, "member diversity is lost") has to be redone. Some of those conclusions
may be artifacts of members collapsing at different random epochs rather than real consequences of
4x the training data. Verdict: they are not. Ratio 2.55 vs 2.28 (paired p 0.58) and ICC 0.187 vs
0.192 both survive with no collapsed members; the consistent under-confidence does not.

672hr_stable boot01 result (2026-10-27, wandb k5gwnlfb, L40S, 6h21m for 10 epochs)

Ran under the 10-epoch configs now preserved in configs_bootstrap/672hr_stable.10ep.20261027/.
The live config set is 6 epochs -- see below for why.

ep  train loss  val loss  val/train  val IoU  gnorm med  gnorm max
 0     0.00870   0.00990       1.14   0.5572      0.017      0.570
 1     0.00530   0.01040       1.96   0.5771      0.014      0.070
 2     0.00444   0.01120       2.52   0.5683      0.014      0.049
 3     0.00411   0.01070       2.60   0.5798      0.014      0.113
 4     0.00406   0.01020       2.51   0.5798      0.013      0.115
 5     0.00320   0.01160       3.62   0.5921      0.012      0.058
 6     0.00259   0.01280       4.95   0.5952      0.010      0.060
 7     0.00223   0.01330       5.96   0.5926      0.010      0.096
 8     0.00203   0.01380       6.79   0.6016      0.008      0.048   <- best.pth (by valid/iou)
 9     0.00202   0.01410       6.98   0.6015      0.007      0.031

VERDICT: no collapse -- the stabilization worked -- but the model is mildly OVERTRAINED, and in a
different way from 2016hr. 2016hr diverged, with train and val loss rising together. Here a real
generalization gap opens: train loss falls 4.3x while val loss RISES 1.42x, and the val/train
ratio climbs 1.14 -> 6.98 monotonically. That is textbook overfitting. valid/loss bottoms out at
epoch 0 and rises steadily from epoch 4. Meanwhile IoU plateaus: the last four epochs span
0.5926-0.6016, flat within noise, and epoch 9 is -0.0001 against epoch 8. Not undertrained --
nothing suggests more epochs help. IoU creeps up while the probabilities get worse because BCE
punishes confident errors and a 0.5 threshold does not care about confidence magnitude.

THE FIX BOUGHT ROBUSTNESS, NOT ACCURACY. Against old 672hr boot01 (flat lr 1e-3):
                    old 672hr          new 672hr_stable
  peak IoU          0.6036 (ep2)       0.6016 (ep8)
  val loss at peak  0.0088             0.0138
  final IoU (ep9)   0.4169             0.6015
Both hit IoU 0.5572 at epoch 0 (same seed, same replicate; warmup touches only 70 of 672 steps).
The old run climbed faster and peaked both higher AND better calibrated at epoch 2, then destroyed
itself at epoch 5 (IoU 0.2284, val loss 0.4144). The new run is slower, steadier, and lands at a
statistically indistinguishable peak. So the gain is a usable final checkpoint (+0.185 IoU) and no
more best-epoch archaeology -- not a higher ceiling. Awkward corollary: the single best boot01
checkpoint in existence is still the OLD run's epoch_2.pth (IoU 0.6036, val loss 0.0088).

IMPLICATION FOR THE UQ WORK: BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME selects on IoU, which here picks epoch 8 -- the
second-worst-calibrated checkpoint of the run. Overfit members saturate their probabilities toward
0/1, which shrinks inter-member disagreement. That is a plausible contributor to the documented
"ensemble spread understates error by 2.2-2.3x": the spread may be tight partly because members
are individually overconfident, not because the prediction is good. Hypothesis consistent with
this run, not proven by it. finetune.py now takes optimizer.best_metric (valid/iou default,
valid/dice or valid/loss accepted) so the criterion can be switched; and because every epoch_N.pth
is kept, selection can also be redone post-hoc without retraining.

WHY THE LIVE SET IS 6 EPOCHS: epochs 6-9 cost ~1.6 TB and ~2.5 h each for +0.006 IoU (noise) and
+10% val loss. Cutting to 6 saves ~14 TB and ~23 h across the nine remaining members. A 6-epoch
config is NOT the same as stopping a 10-epoch run at 6 -- the cosine total is max_epochs x 672, so
6 epochs anneals to min_lr by epoch 6 instead of sitting near 3.5e-4 there, and should land
slightly better than this run's epoch 6 did. boot01 therefore needs re-running at 6 epochs before
it joins members 2-10 in a BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME entry; mixing budgets is what made the old 672hr
set (boot01 at 10, the rest at 5) hard to compare.

672hr_stable full ensemble (2026-10-29, all 10 members at 6 epochs, L40S, 3.5-4.0 h each)

Members, in wandb: boot01 j6huxcrn, boot02 s96wimmi, boot03 9ei4z7gv, boot04 vivfkjiv,
boot05 wacgmsao, boot06 tcu7qblb, boot07 f76zjsdj, boot08 rkbzguhx, boot09 s9vokocg,
boot10 bxx09je1. One clean sequential pass of run_bootstrap_ensemble_672hr_stable.sh, members
1 to 10, ending "All 10 members complete." (run_bootstrap_ensemble_672hr_stable_rerun.log).

PROVENANCE OF THE TWO EXTRA RUNS. k5gwnlfb (boot01, 10 epochs, the entry above) and 35u5h5xg
(boot02) are NOT members. After k5gwnlfb's result came in, the 10-epoch pass was interrupted 365
batches into boot02's epoch 0 at 21:19:07 and relaunched from member 1 at 21:23 under the 6-epoch
configs. The SignalException traceback in run_bootstrap_ensemble_672hr_stable.log is signal 2 --
that deliberate SIGINT, not a failure. 35u5h5xg wrote no checkpoint: boot02/ holds only
epoch_0-5.pth, all timestamped after j6huxcrn finished.

Mean over the ten members. Read from each run's .wandb datastore, not output.log, which rounds
Avg Loss to 4 dp -- enough to tie boot03's epochs 2 and 3 at 0.0073 when full precision separates
them 0.007265 vs 0.007322. The datastore lays each history item out as
0x12 <len> <key> 0x82 0x01 <len> <value_json>.

ep  train loss  val loss  val/train  val IoU          gnorm med  gnorm max
 0     0.02614   0.00991       0.38   0.5396+-0.0298      0.020      1.585
 1     0.00645   0.00896       1.39   0.5831+-0.0282      0.015      0.150
 2     0.00528   0.00881       1.67   0.6007+-0.0252      0.013      0.105   <- val loss floor
 3     0.00448   0.00882       1.97   0.6150+-0.0259      0.013      0.128
 4     0.00377   0.00904       2.40   0.6242+-0.0233      0.012      0.104   <- IoU plateau
 5     0.00334   0.00941       2.82   0.6254+-0.0250      0.010      0.123

VERDICT: boot01's diagnosis generalizes to all ten, milder. NOT UNDERTRAINED -- IoU increments
decay 0.0435, 0.0175, 0.0143, 0.0092, 0.0012, and the epoch 4 -> 5 gain fails a paired t-test
(t(9)=0.67, 5/10 members improve) where 3 -> 4 passes (+0.0092, t=3.12). IoU saturates at epoch 4.
k5gwnlfb's four extra epochs bought +0.001 IoU for +42% val loss, so nothing suggests a longer
budget helps. MILDLY OVERTRAINED probabilistically -- mean val loss bottoms at epoch 2 and is
significantly worse by epoch 5 (paired t(9)=3.17); per member the rise from its own floor is
+0.0% to +21.4%, mean +10.9%; train loss falls monotonically throughout and val/train climbs
0.38 -> 2.82. Not divergence: see the clipping amendment above.

THE HEADLINE IS THAT THE TWO SELECTION CRITERIA DISAGREE.
  best valid/iou epoch   {ep3: 1, ep4: 4, ep5: 5}
  min valid/loss epoch   {ep0: 1, ep1: 1, ep2: 4, ep3: 2, ep4: 1, ep5: 1}
best.pth tracks valid/iou, so for 9 of 10 members it lands 2-3 epochs past that member's
calibration optimum. boot07 is the lone exception: its val loss is still falling at epoch 5, so
both criteria pick ep5 there. Selecting on min valid/loss instead costs 0.0168 mean validation
IoU. Every epoch_N.pth is retained, so that re-selection needs no retraining -- the loss-optimal
epochs are boot01 ep0, boot02 ep4, boot03 ep2, boot04 ep2, boot05 ep3, boot06 ep2, boot07 ep5,
boot08 ep2, boot09 ep3, boot10 ep1.

Per-member, IoU by epoch 0-5, with the selected epoch in parentheses:
  boot01  .5595 .5744 .5624 .5854 .5971 .6006  (ep5)    boot06  .5593 .5803 .6104 .6117 .6177 .6178  (ep5)
  boot02  .5112 .5480 .5593 .5771 .6018 .6015  (ep4)    boot07  .5631 .6226 .6245 .6441 .6483 .6597  (ep5)
  boot03  .5312 .5582 .6279 .6491 .6538 .6574  (ep5)    boot08  .5265 .5488 .5880 .5958 .5968 .5964  (ep4)
  boot04  .5595 .6142 .6223 .6166 .6374 .6452  (ep5)    boot09  .4851 .5760 .6018 .6011 .6170 .6162  (ep4)
  boot05  .5821 .6208 .6203 .6481 .6573 .6500  (ep4)    boot10  .5190 .5879 .5898 .6208 .6147 .6091  (ep3)

Selected-epoch IoU spans 0.5968-0.6597, mean 0.6274, sd 0.0252 -- 21x the epoch 4 -> 5 gain and
12x the mean best-minus-final gap of 0.0020. Which replicate you trained on matters far more than
which epoch you stop at. That is the right behaviour for a bootstrap, but see the seed-ablation
entry below for what that spread leaves out.

A SYSTEMATIC BIAS WORTH TRACKING: at the selected epoch, recall exceeds precision in 9 of 10
members (means 0.796 vs 0.748) and predicted AR area runs ~7% above truth (ratio 0.98-1.14). The
ensemble over-segments. This is bias, not variance, so no amount of member spread will expose it
and no epoch re-selection will remove it -- relevant to the 2.28x error/uncertainty ratio, since
members that all over-segment by 7% agree with each other and all differ from truth.

Against the earlier regimes, on the same 200-frame validation set:
                     members   best-epoch IoU     last-epoch IoU     val loss floor -> last
  168hrs (10 ep)        9      0.5110+-0.0239     0.4926+-0.0339     +23%
  672hr (5 ep)          9      0.5902+-0.0394     0.3335+-0.2076     -> 13.6  (diverged)
  672hr_stable (6 ep)  10      0.6274+-0.0252     0.6254+-0.0250     +10.7%
First regime where no member collapses: every run's last epoch is within 0.012 IoU of its own
peak, so "best epoch" barely selects at all. Also the highest-scoring and tightest-spread.

Three footnotes on that table. The val loss column is a ratio of ensemble means, not a mean of
per-member ratios -- the two differ, cf. the +10.9% above. The 168hrs and 672hr rows are
log-derived at 4 dp, so +23% is good to about half a point. And both earlier rows hold nine
members, not ten, for different reasons: 672hr boot01 (nb9lm7a4) ran 10 epochs against the
others' 5, a non-comparable budget; 168hrs boot01 (b919pvy1) ran 20 and predates the per-regime
config directories -- its --config_path is the flat ./configs_bootstrap/config_boot01.yaml -- so
it does not group with the rest by path either. Both are in BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME regardless,
where only the peak epoch matters.

WHAT THE VALIDATION SET CANNOT TELL US. 200 frames over 9 days (Jan 15 of 2011-2019),
day-disjoint from every replicate -- no leakage, verified -- but small, heavily autocorrelated
(hourly frames within 9 solar configurations), and also the epoch-selection set, so 0.6254 is
selection-biased, if only mildly (mean best-minus-final 0.0020). Generalization proper needs the
holdout cohort -- run 2026-10-29, next section.

HOUSEKEEPING DONE. boot01/ held epoch_6-9.pth from k5gwnlfb (6720-step cosine) alongside
epoch_0-5.pth from j6huxcrn (4032-step cosine) -- 19 GB against 12 GB for every other member, and
a live hazard for anything selecting by epoch number. Moved to boot01/stale_10ep_k5gwnlfb/ with a
README recording k5gwnlfb's per-epoch IoU.

672hr_stable holdout inference + sections 6/7 (2026-10-29)

view_holdout_nonflaring30_sections67_672hr_stable.ipynb, over the same 30 non-flaring test-era ARs
as the 672hr run, so every comparison below is PAIRED per AR (Wilcoxon signed-rank on the
differences). The between-frame sd of the ratio is 1.3 in both runs, far larger than any shift
between regimes, so unpaired cohort means would resolve nothing. The notebook asserts the pairing
by checking each AR's ground-truth positive fraction -- a property of the masks alone -- is
bit-identical across the two runs; max |difference| is exactly 0.

Provenance: BEST_EPOCH_BY_REGIME["672hr_stable"] added from the 60 "Validation -- IoU:" lines of
run_bootstrap_ensemble_672hr_stable_rerun.log (ep5/4/5/5/4/5/5/4/4/3 for boot01-10); 300 inferences
on a T4 in ~60 min into .../672hr_stable_infer_holdout_nonflaring30, resuming a partial pass that
had already written all 30 channels.nc + prob_boot01 and 24 prob_boot02, so that root's
prob attrs carry a RELATIVE source_file ("./assets/holdout_nc_nonflaring30/...") -- the analysis
script's plate_scale() reads it, so analyze_holdout_sections67.py must be run from
downstream_examples/ar_segmentation/ for this root. --check-best-epoch reported no mismatches, no
frame failed the fp16 mean-is-a-mean check, and no member predicted an empty mask.

                          672hr_stable      672hr    paired p   note
  error / uncertainty     2.55 +- 1.39   2.28 +- 1.31   0.58    median delta -0.01, 15/30 improved
  cohort mean-ERR/mean-UNC       2.50x          1.79x     --    UNC's tail shrank, ERR did not
  UNCERTAINTY ASSD (px)           9.73          13.97    0.10    median delta only -0.40 px
  ERROR ASSD (px)                24.32          24.96    0.080   error HD95 166.6 vs 166.5
  soft-mean IoU                 0.7407         0.7115   2.6e-07  up on 29/30
  soft recall                   0.8305         0.7904   3.7e-09  precision -0.0065 (p 0.070)
  ICC (frame identity)           0.187          0.192     --     sigma_b 4.62 vs 16.51
  Kendall's W                    0.104          0.331     --     Friedman p 8.9e-04
  Method A vs B, Pearson r       0.386          0.053     --     p 0.035 vs 0.78
  pooled bias (p-bar - obs)    -0.0069        -0.0311     --     per-AR +0.0021, 17 under/13 over
  pooled ECE                    0.0138         0.0317     --     per-AR 0.0585 vs 0.0604 (p 0.37)
  Brier, per AR                 0.1168         0.1230    0.003   better on 23/30
  missed AR fraction            0.0629         0.0682    0.011   k=0 still hides 6.3 +- 2.0 %

FOUR THINGS THIS SETTLES.

1. The 2.28x was NOT an artifact of collapsed members. Paired p 0.58, median per-AR change -0.01.
   Every detection failure is gone -- 4 of 300 member-frames exceeded 100 px ASSD in the old run
   (max 434.8), 0 of 300 do now (max 97.9) -- and the spread is still only half the error.

2. The ICC prediction in this file was WRONG. The earlier entry read 0.192 as "driven by the heavy
   tail of those three members, not by uniform scatter", which predicts a higher ICC once they are
   gone. Both variance components fell ~3.5x and the ratio did not move (0.187). The 81% members /
   19% frame-identity split is a real property of bootstrapping this model.

3. The IoU gain does NOT reach the boundary. +0.0292 IoU on 29/30 ARs (p 2.6e-07), driven by recall
   (+0.0401) with precision slightly down -- the over-segmentation this file flagged at training
   time, confirmed on holdout -- while ERR and error HD95 do not move. Pixel overlap and boundary
   localization are different axes. Tightening UNC 30% while ERR stands still makes the error bar
   WORSE, which is why the cohort-level ratio rose 1.79x -> 2.50x.

4. The probability-saturation prediction in this file was RIGHT, and it helped. Mean p-bar rose on
   30/30 ARs (+0.0546, p 1.9e-09), exactly as predicted for best.pth checkpoints 2-3 epochs past
   their validation-loss optimum. But the old ensemble was UNDER-confident, so the extra confidence
   landed on the diagonal: pooled bias -0.0311 -> -0.0069, Murphy reliability 0.0015 -> 0.0004.
   The cost is that the sign consistency is gone (17 under / 13 over, Wilcoxon p 0.52, against
   23/30 at p 1.5e-3), so the old notebook's "threshold below 0.5" recommendation NO LONGER HOLDS.
   Per-AR |bias| and ECE improvements are not significant (p 0.16, p 0.37); Brier's is.

ONE NEW RESULT the old cohort could not produce: pairing each member against ITS OWN replicate
across the two optimizer schedules gives Spearman +0.733 (p 0.016) on mean ASSD. A replicate that
yielded a loose member yields a loose one again when retrained. Replicate identity transfers;
per-frame member ranking does not (W 0.104). The old W of 0.331 was mostly three broken
checkpoints being reliably bad.

WATCH OUT FOR ONE FRAME. 20200607_0100 (HARP 7419) has truth_frac 0.0012 -- 0.19x the training-era
rate, 17x sparser than the cohort median -- and carries the worst bias (+0.2648), Brier (0.2035)
and ECE (0.2651) of the 30. It is the only frame whose truth mask is nearly empty while the
ensemble still calls 1.0% of the disk an AR, and it single-handedly pulls the per-AR mean bias
positive. Read medians for calibration on this cohort.

NOT DONE, in priority order: (a) re-run this cohort at each member's MIN-VAL-LOSS epoch
(boot01 ep0, boot02 ep4, boot03 ep2, boot04 ep2, boot05 ep3, boot06 ep2, boot07 ep5, boot08 ep2,
boot09 ep3, boot10 ep1) into the already-created
.../672hr_stable_minloss_infer_holdout_nonflaring30 -- the direct test of point 4, ~60 min T4, no
retraining since every epoch_N.pth is kept; (b) HARP 11930 under 672hr_stable, so the single-frame
reference markers in the notebooks stop being cross-regime (3 frames, ~8 min); (c) the 168hrs
cohort, one --regime flag.

Seed ablation: the variance component none of these ensembles sample (run 2026-10-29/30)

make_seed_ablation_configs.py + configs_bootstrap/seed_ablation/ + run_seed_ablation.sh, with the
design and caveats in configs_bootstrap/seed_ablation/README.md. RUN AND ANSWERED. Both arms
completed 6 epochs on replicate train_boot001.csv -- seed01 = bm06c9dt, seed02 = x7m7u8fz -- and
both printed the "Seed N (torch/numpy/random 42+N, train sampler N)" line, so commit b47cb54's
plumbing worked. seed00 is 672hr_stable/boot01 (j6huxcrn) by design, not a re-run; all three
configs under configs_bootstrap/seed_ablation/ name the same ar_index_train, verified.

ONE EARLIER RUN IS NOT AN ARM. re5vbc9l (13:55) printed "Clipping gradients" but NO Seed line --
the training box was still on a pre-seed finetune.py -- and was killed with KeyboardInterrupt. It
logged zero validations and wrote no checkpoint. grep -c 'Validation --' is 6 / 6 / 0 for
bm06c9dt / x7m7u8fz / re5vbc9l.

Every number below is re-derived from the .wandb datastores rather than output.log, which rounds
loss to 4 dp, and cross-checked against each run's six "Validation -- IoU:" lines; all 13 runs
used here (3 arms + 10 replicates) agree exactly. best.pth mtimes independently confirm each
selection: seed01's (18:15) follows epoch_5.pth (18:09), seed02's (20:19) falls between
epoch_2.pth (20:11) and epoch_3.pth (20:53).

THE QUESTION THIS WAS BUILT TO ANSWER, ANSWERED: NO, THE FIXED SEED IS NOT WHY THE ENSEMBLE
SPREAD UNDERSTATES ERROR.

  ep   across-seed sd   across-replicate sd   seed var share   sd ratio
   0      0.00828             0.02979              7.2%          0.278
   1      0.01062             0.02816             12.4%          0.377
   2      0.02162             0.02519             42.4%          0.858   <- seed spread peaks
   3      0.00530             0.02587              4.0%          0.205
   4      0.00725             0.02333              8.8%          0.311
   5      0.00161             0.02501              0.4%          0.064   <- arms converge

Pooling that across epochs is the whole ballgame, and the three defensible choices disagree:

                        seed sd          rep sd        sd ratio   seed var share
  ep0-5 (all)        0.01105 (df 12)  0.02631 (df 54)    0.420        15.0%
  ep3-5 (deployed)   0.00527 (df  6)  0.02476 (df 27)    0.213         4.3%
  ep0-2              0.01471 (df  6)  0.02778 (df 27)    0.529        21.9%

ep3-5 IS THE RIGHT ESTIMAND. The ten replicates' selected epochs are ep3-5 (ep3: 1, ep4: 4,
ep5: 5), so that is the range members are actually deployed from. ep0-2 mixes in under-trained
checkpoints nobody deploys and inflates the seed share to 21.9%; pooling all six epochs splits
the difference at 15.0% for the same reason. Both are averaging over TRAINING STAGES, not over
seeds -- an early-epoch checkpoint is more seed-sensitive because it has had less time to
converge, which is a fact about epoch 0, not about the ensemble that ships.

THE ARITHMETIC, and it is not close. If the two components are independent the total sd is
sqrt(sd_seed^2 + sd_rep^2), so adding the seed component inflates the recorded across-replicate
spread by a factor that then divides the error/uncertainty ratio (exact for the cohort-level
mean-ERR/mean-UNC, which scales inversely with UNC while ERR is untouched):
  point estimate  sd_seed 0.00527 -> inflate 1.0224x -> 2.55x becomes 2.49x
  95% upper bound sd_seed 0.01010 -> inflate 1.0799x -> 2.55x becomes 2.36x
(one-sided 95%, chi2 on 6 df, seed var share 14.2%; the two-sided 0.025 bound of 0.01161 gives
2.31x). Even at the upper bound, seed diversity moves a 2.55x gap to 2.36x. It cannot close it.

THE SHARPER FINDING UNDERNEATH: DIFFERENT SEEDS CONVERGE, DIFFERENT REPLICATES DO NOT.
  seed00  .5595 .5744 .5624 .5854 .5971 .6006
  seed01  .5431 .5832 .5790 .5894 .5982 .6004
  seed02  .5495 .5956 .6053 .5959 .5851 .5977
At epoch 5 the three arms sit at 0.6006 / 0.6004 / 0.5977, sd 0.00161, while the ten replicates
span 0.0633 (0.5964-0.6597, sd 0.02501) -- a 0.41% variance share, F = 241 on (9,2) df. The
training-loss curves are nearly indistinguishable too: epoch_loss max/min - 1 runs +1.8%, +5.3%,
+2.0%, +1.1%, +2.3%, +1.5% over epochs 0-5, i.e. within 2.4% at five of six epochs, despite a
different LoRA init AND a different sample order.

But they DIVERGE MID-RUN. Across-seed sd peaks at 0.0216 at epoch 2 -- 0.858 of the replicate sd
there, essentially replicate-level -- and seed02 peaks early (0.6053 at ep2), dips to 0.5851, and
recovers to 0.5977. The seed moves the PATH, not the DESTINATION. That is what a rank-8 adapter
perturbing 0.51% of parameters around one frozen pretrained point should do, and it
retroactively VALIDATES fixing the seed for the bootstrap -- the choice this ablation existed to
question. It also means a seed-diverse ensemble would have to be stopped mid-run to buy any
diversity at all, at exactly the epochs with the worst IoU.

TWO STANDING CLAIMS IN THIS FILE GET SEED-INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION.
  * OVER-SEGMENTATION IS STRUCTURAL. At epoch 5 the pred/true AR area ratio is 1.100 / 1.119 /
    1.102 and recall exceeds precision in 3/3 arms, matching 9/10 replicates (at each arm's own
    selected epoch: 1.100 / 1.119 / 1.078, recall > precision 3/3). It survives resampling on
    BOTH axes, so no ensembling scheme over either can expose it. This is the strongest support
    available for point 3 above -- that the remaining gap is bias. NB these ratios are
    full-precision; the log's 4-dp AR-fraction field reads them as 1.109 / 1.125 / 1.109, so the
    ~7%-above-truth figure quoted earlier in this file is a 4-dp artifact of the same kind.
  * THE OVERFITTING IS NOT AN ARTIFACT OF SEED 42, and is worse without it. Validation loss
    floors at ep0 / ep1 / ep2 and rises +14.2% / +27.3% / +34.7% to ep5.

Also: seed01 put one epoch-0 batch at grad norm 1.8882, making it a FIFTH run on this recipe to
engage the 1.0 clip during warmup (4 of 10 672hr_stable members did), further supporting the
clipping amendment above. seed00 and seed02 top out at 0.5696 and 0.9219 and never engage it.

AND A CAUTIONARY ONE. seed02's best.pth is epoch 2, chosen over its own epoch 5 by 0.0076 IoU
(0.6053 vs 0.5977). On a 200-frame validation set that is selection noise. It is the cleanest
illustration in this repo of why IoU-based epoch picking is fragile: the same recipe, perturbed
only in its seed, hands best.pth a mid-run checkpoint whose advantage would not survive a
different validation draw. Both other arms pick ep5.

Why: model uncertainty splits into a finite-sample component (which training draw you got) and an
optimization-path component (which basin SGD found). The bootstrap ensembles vary the first and
hold the second fixed -- all ten 672hr_stable members share one seed, hence one LoRA init and one
training-sample order -- so member spread omits the second entirely. Together with the
overfitting above that is two independent reasons the spread understates error, and only the
overfitting one is addressable by re-selecting epochs. This ablation sizes the other.

Design: one replicate (train_boot001.csv), seeds 0/1/2, derived from
configs_bootstrap/672hr_stable/config_boot01.yaml so each arm differs from boot01 in exactly
{seed, job_id, path_experiment} by construction rather than by re-deriving a field list.

TWO FACTS THAT SHAPED IT, both verified:
  * seed 0 is a bit-for-bit no-op -- set_global_seed(0) is manual_seed(42), and the sampler's
    default seed is already 0 -- so 672hr_stable/boot01 IS the seed-0 arm. Only seeds 1 and 2
    need training, ~3.7 h each. config_seed00.yaml is generated anyway so the claim is checkable;
    run_seed_ablation.sh skips it.
  * the global seed does NOT reach the data order. DistributedSampler shuffles from a private
    torch.Generator seeded with sampler_seed + epoch, untouched by the global RNG, so
    set_global_seed moves only lora_A's init and the dropout masks. finetune.py now hands `seed`
    to the train sampler as well (commit b47cb54). Varying only the global seed would have left
    the sample order identical across arms and understated the very thing being measured.
The validation sampler is deliberately left unseeded: the loop scores a fixed
iters_per_epoch_valid prefix of it, so reseeding would change which frames are scored and break
comparability with all 30 existing members. Related: finetune.py never calls sampler.set_epoch(),
so every epoch within a run iterates in the same order. Left alone on purpose -- changing it
would alter training dynamics and break that same comparability.

Reading it: compare the across-seed sd of validation IoU against the across-replicate sd of
0.0252 above. Much smaller means the existing ensemble already captures nearly all the variance
and the 2.28x gap is mostly bias; comparable means the ensemble is missing roughly half the
variance and a crossed seeds x replicates grid is warranted. n=3 puts an sd on 2 df, 95% CI
~0.52x-3.7x, so this detects an order-of-magnitude difference and not a factor of 1.5 -- add
seeds rather than conclude if it lands borderline. The design is L-shaped, not crossed: ten
replicates at one seed plus three seeds at one replicate share the single cell (boot01, seed 0),
which supports comparing marginal variances but not an interaction term. And neither component
sees bias: all arms share the frozen surya.366m.v1.pt, so common-mode error in that
representation is invisible to every spread estimator here.
